## What I checked, and how

- **PDF** (`report/report.pdf`): 2 pages, US letter, pdfTeX. Rendered both pages and read them in full. The
  default anonymous submission mode is used (line numbers, "Anonymous Author(s)", the template's submission footer).
  Exactly one figure and one table. Fonts are embedded (Type 1 for the text, TrueType from the figure, no Type 3).
- **Template**: `report/neurips_2026.sty` is byte-identical (md5 f447d3302c8719cb27619a074c876b44) to the style file
  in the official https://media.neurips.cc/Conferences/NeurIPS2026/Formatting_Instructions_For_NeurIPS_2026.zip,
  downloaded independently.
- **Build**: `latexmk -pdf` (TeX Live 2026) on a copy of the assembled `report/` rebuilds a 2-page PDF whose text is
  identical to the delivered PDF. No undefined references or citations, no overfull boxes.
- **Data (A1/D1)**: `fetch_data.py` re-run here. It fetched OpenML 61 v1 from openml.org, and its md5
  ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9 matches OpenML's checksum and the delivered `code/data/iris.arff` (cmp identical).
  The 3 duplicate rows match the brief.
- **Methods/protocol (B1-B3, S1-S3)**: read `cv_compare.py`.
  - LR: lbfgs with C=1 inside a StandardScaler pipeline, which is multinomial for 3 classes in sklearn 1.5.2.
  - kNN: StandardScaler and kNN inside GridSearchCV, k in 1..30, inner stratified 5-fold on the outer training set
    only; ties go to the smallest k.
  - Outer splits: RepeatedStratifiedKFold(5, 10, seed 20261017), giving 50 splits shared by both methods (paired).
- **Independent re-run**: full CV in a fresh Python 3.11.17 env with the pinned `requirements.txt`. My `folds.csv`
  (md5 d5f5a84454df539df6e51082b9c727c2) and `predictions.csv` (md5 83320bcb576b3bb595c596cdddc3cd03) are
  byte-identical to both delivered runs, so a third environment gives the same results. Re-running `compare_runs.py`
  and `summarize.py` reproduces `numbers.tex`, `table.csv`, `folds_long.csv` and `summary.json`; the only
  differences are my own host name and Python version.
- **Statistics (C1-C3, S4-S5)**: corrected resampled t (Nadeau-Bengio, ratio 30/120, df 49), applied to each method
  and to the paired per-fold differences. Checked by hand:
  - difference SD 3.30 pp gives a half-width of 2.010 x sqrt(0.27) x 3.30 = 3.45, so 0.93 [-2.51, 4.38];
    corrected-t p = 0.59.
  - per-method intervals: LR 95.4 [91.6, 99.2], kNN 94.5 [89.5, 99.5].
  - folds: 16 better / 25 tied / 9 worse, matching the bubble counts (1+4+11 / 25 / 9).
  - selected k: median 8, mode 9.

  The naive interval is shown only as a labelled contrast. Claims stay within the intervals: "no detectable
  difference", explicitly not equivalence.
- **Ibex vs local (D1-D2, S6-S8)**: confirmed with SLURM's own accounting on Ibex
  (`sacct -j 53912439`): JobName rw-1509e8c38753-iris-cv, partition batch, NodeList cn113-35-l, COMPLETED,
  AllocTRES cpu=1,mem=2G,node=1 (no GPU), end 2026-10-17T16:26:10 local, which is 13:26:10Z and matches `run.json`.
  The node name in the paper (cn113-35-l) matches the job log (SLURMD_NODENAME) and the accounting. The paper states
  the identity criterion (byte identity of per-fold and per-case files plus exact equality of every value); 0/50
  folds and 0/6000 predictions differ (1500 test cases x 4 prediction columns). Software versions are given for
  both environments.
- **Table**: `tables/make_table.py` re-run regenerates `table_results.tex` identically and asserts agreement with
  `results/table.csv`.
- **Figure**: `figures/make_figure.py` re-run succeeds. I inspected the figure at 250 dpi in the PDF. Both panels
  are labelled (axis titles with units, method tick labels, panel titles), with distinct markers as well as colour,
  and they show what the text and caption say: per-fold accuracies with mean and corrected interval, and the
  paired-difference distribution with the mean, interval and zero line.
- **Numbers in text**: every number is a macro from `results/numbers.tex`, produced by `code/summarize.py`; no number
  is typed by hand. References checked: Fisher 1936, Vanschoren et al. 2013, Nadeau & Bengio 2003,
  Bouckaert & Frank 2004; bibliographic details are correct.
- **Venue (V1-V9)**: all met. The NeurIPS checklist is omitted under the objective's 2-page cap, as venue-repo
  decided, and the paper discloses this.

## Non-blocking observations (no action required)

1. The figure caption describes the panels but has no take-away sentence, which venue.md suggests. The take-away
   is carried by the text, so this is a style point.
2. The limitation that the duplicate rows "slightly favour nearest-neighbour methods" is qualitative and was not
   measured. It is hedged and placed among the limitations, as the brief recommends.
3. venue.md V3 says "footer absent", but the unmodified official style prints the "Submitted to ... NeurIPS 2026"
   footer in submission mode. The delivered PDF follows the official template, so the footer is correct.

## Conclusion

The delivery meets every requirement of objective #1 and every item of `review/standard.md`, and every reported
number reproduces independently. Approved for release.
